# All text must stay LF in the working tree. gofmt reports CRLF .go files as
# unformatted, and a CRLF `*.sh` fails `bash -n` (and `#!/bin/bash\r` is a
# broken shebang on Linux). r7 found several upstream text files still had
# CRLF in a Windows checkout because core.autocrlf=true restored it; the
# extra patterns below close that gap for every tracked text type.
# Git blobs are already LF — these attributes only control checkout.
# A default rule also covers future file types (.json, .toml, extensionless
# files). `text=auto` keeps real binaries untouched. The attributes file
# itself needs an explicit rule: otherwise autocrlf=true rewrites it on the
# next checkout and the whole guard becomes CRLF on a Windows machine.
*              text=auto eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
*.go   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
